course: dictionary
lesson: 10934
title: Voyager: a word that travels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you meet a new word, look at the words around it. «Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, launched in 1977, flew past the giant planets.» The capital V and the number after it tell you that Voyager is a name, so it is a spacecraft, not a person.
explain: «Seen from Voyager 1 six billion kilometres away, the whole Earth was a pale blue dot.» Six billion kilometres is an enormous distance, so the clues tell you the probe has gone very far. A voyager is a person on a long journey, so the word fits the idea of travelling far.
explain: Now try the meaning in another sentence: «Polynesian voyagers sailed across oceans using the stars.» Swap in "a person on a long journey" and check: it still makes sense. When the sentence still makes sense with your meaning, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A voyager is a person who goes on a long journey, especially by sea or into space.
fact: With a capital V and a number, as in Voyager 1, the word is the name of a space probe.
fact: Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 were launched in 1977, and they have now travelled further than anything else people have made.
fact: Voyager 2 is the only spacecraft to have visited Uranus and Neptune, in 1986 and 1989.
fact: The probes carry a Golden Record with sounds and pictures of Earth, in case aliens ever find them.
exercise: «Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 flew past the giant planets.» What kind of thing is Voyager 1 here? | A king who lived on a hill | A kind of planet | *A space probe | A ship sailing across an ocean
exercise: Polynesian voyagers found New Zealand and Hawaii using the stars. What is a voyager? | A bright star in the sky | A wide, flat beach | A person who never leaves home | *A person on a long journey, often by sea
exercise: «The Golden Record travels on Voyager 2.» What does the capital V in Voyager tell you? | *It is a name, so it means that spacecraft | It means a long sea journey | Nothing, it is a typing mistake | It starts a new sentence
test: «Seen from Voyager 1 six billion kilometres away, the whole Earth was a pale blue dot.» What does six billion kilometres suggest? | Very close to home | *A very great distance | A lot of rain | A very short walk
test: «Uranus and Neptune have each been visited only once, by Voyager 2.» What does only tell you? | Voyager 2 is very slow | Voyager 2 went there many times | *Voyager 2 went to each planet just one time | Voyager 2 never went anywhere
exam: «Jupiter has faint, thin rings made of dust, discovered by Voyager 1 in 1979.» What does faint mean? | Very loud and noisy | *Very weak, hard to see | Very bright and shiny | Very large and heavy
